  "worked_solution": "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-2)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-2)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi (t+2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi t+4\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi t+2\\cdot 2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+2)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
